ge.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Anchor B can now land late: a version tag on an unmerged line forked near the tip narrows the walk", "recommendation": "instance. Why: since the 2026-11-26 amendment, _tag_candidate takes the newest version-shaped tag from all of refs/tags, and B is the merge-base of that tag with the base. A tag on a branch that forked near the base's tip makes that merge-base near the tip, so the anchor moves late and the walk shrinks. That branch might never ship (an rc tag on a release branch still in test, or a stray v-tag on a feature branch). The plan's tradeoff and release_anchor's docstring name only the widening case (an old hotfix tag). Under Chunk 06's gate, a shrunken walk lets a release pass over work no review read. Before Chunk 06, either limit B to tags reachable from a release ref, or have the owner accept this as a named tradeoff.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "_resolve_anchor drops the release-file candidate when the tag is nearer and _oldest_containing fails", "recommendation": "instance. Take this path: the tag is nearer, so chosen stays None from the release-file branch, and _oldest_containing then returns None (a git failure). The result is no anchor, and the stated reason says no release file exists, which is false. The docstring claims 'a failure only ever widens the walk', but here the walk is skipped entirely. A per_release gate would then refuse (fail closed), but the advisory report misstates why. Fix: fall back to the release-file anchor when B cannot be resolved. A monkeypatched failure of _oldest_containing pins it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/boundary_coverage.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "boundary_review_policy's 'on the integration branch' typo note has no test", "recommendation": "instance. Every typo test writes the same line on both sides, so the working-tree note branch always an